  "query_preview": "..\nSatellite imagery this morning showed partly to \nmostly cloudy conditions. At the surface, outflow winds from NM \nstorms last night has pushed surface dewpoints into the mid 40s \nto lower 50s as far west far eastern Pinal/Pima counties. PWATs,\nper satellite, have increased to over 1\" east of Tucson while \nlower values less than 1\" were from Tucson west. For today, \nisolated to scattered thunderstorms will occur Greenlee, Graham \nand Cochise counties. However, some CAMS has storms as far west \ninto far eastern Pima county and Santa Cruz county. Something \nfor dayshift to monitor. Main concern from storms today will be \nstrong gusty winds up 50+ mph and blowing dust. May also see \nsome blowing dust moving into Cochise county this evening from \nthunderstorms in Hidalgo and Grant counties in NM. \n\nAfter today, moisture values will slowly increase as the week\nprogresses along with daily westward expansion of afternoon and\nevening showers and thunderstorms. Gusty winds/blowing dust main\nconcerns Tue/Wed with flash flooding threat increasing late in\nthe week. \n\nMeanwhile, as is often the case transitioning into a more classic \nmonsoon pattern, we do have some heat to deal with. High \ntemperatures will peak Tuesday and Wednesday about 3 to 8 degrees \nabove normal. With areas of major HeatRisk in the lower elevations \nfrom Tucson westward and in Graham County along the Gila River, \nan Extreme Heat Warning is in effect Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nFun fact: Today at 10:30 am, the earth will be at aphelion or\nthe farthest away from the sun at 94,502,962 miles.",
